  "title": "The Clapper",
  "year": "1984",
  "subtitle": "Clap-on, clap-off — the most iconic gesture-recognition device of the decade, a sound-activated switch that made two sharp handclaps a universal command.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Clapper is the purest, most commercially ubiquitous embodiment of embodied gesture as a computer command before Kinect — no keyboard, no switch, no remote: the human body claps twice and the machine obeys. It made the physical ritual of clapping the entire interface. No other artifact in the museum is a sound-pattern-recognition command device, and none has its pop-cultural reach (immortalized in memes, TV, and as the canonical 'simple interface' joke). It belongs because the interaction model IS the product: two claps in quick succession toggle a mains appliance, turning the body itself into a command channel.",
  "search_terms": ["Clapper", "clap-on clap-off", "sound activated switch", "Joseph Enterprises", "gesture recognition", "1984"],
